{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1521218-a-little-question-about-jade-kindred-of-the-east","id":1521218,"title":"A little question about Jade (Kindred of the East)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-08-22T13:17:18-07:00","id":1521218,"title":"A little question about Jade (Kindred of the East)","content":"Hi.<br /> I am making this thread because I am confused about certain things when it's coming to Jade.<br /> <br /> The corebook of the Kuei-jin states that Jade has multiple types, White being the most common and True Jade being the rarest. Now, the KOTE Companion book seem to elaborate more about different types of jade, though I still am very confused on how can one acquire jade to begin with?<br /> <br /> Additionally, I do not know WtO so well, but to me it does seem like White Jade is essentially Wraiths plasm?<br /> I would be more than grateful if somebody could please explain about Jade, because no matter how far I tried to dig, I did not manage to get the information that I needed.<br /> Thanks in advance!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25177-lirbo","name":"Lirbo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25177_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"48"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-22T22:55:13-07:00","id":1521235,"title":"","content":"Jade is... well... a mineral found in the Middle Kingdom. Jade is an umbrella term that encompasses two different silicate minerals: jadeite and nephrite. Jadeite is the rarer variety and fetches the highest prices. Nephrite occurs more abundantly and usually has more affordable prices. Both types of jade can come in green, red, black, gray, brown, yellow, and white/cream colors. Jadeite can also show lavender, orange, and blue hues. Popularly associated with jade, green colors usually command the highest values. However, every color has the potential for beauty. Generally, blue is the color of jade that is the rarest because it comes from only a few deposits found in Guatemala, Japan and Myanmar. Green and lavender jade are more common.<br /> In Kindred of the East, when attuned with a proper ritual, the common jade becomes a living substance capable of holding Yin and Yang Chi.<br /> Of course, different colors of Jade have different properties.<br /> <b>Black Jade</b> (Tortoise Jade) - The Water Jade is attuned to Yin and can hold up to five points of Yin Chi. Artisans often carve it into teardrop shapes or those of Water creatures.<br /> <b>Red Jade</b> (Phoenix Jade) - This is Fire Jade and exemplifies Yang. It can hold up to five points of Yang Chi. It is often carved into fire shapes or images of Phoenixes. It is also just as likely to be left in raw shape with little carving.\u200b<br /> <b>Blue or Green Jade</b> (Dragon Jade) - These colors of jade represent the Element Wood. It can hold both Yang and Yin Chi: up to three points of Yang and two points of Yin. It is often carved into floral shapes or those of animals of the forest.<br /> <b>White Jade </b>(Tiger Jade) - Exemplifying the Element of Metal, Tiger Jade can hold up to three points of Yin and two points of yang Chi. It is often carved into martial shapes and is a popular adornment for pommel grips of weapons, even pistol grips.<br /> <b>Yellow or Grey Jade </b>(\u200bMandarin Jade) - This jade, representing Earth, is balanced and the most valuable because it can hold either type of Chi up to five points. It is often carved to represent noble beasts or the trappings of kingship like fans or scepters.<br /> <b>Yu Ying Jade</b> - The Perfection of Jade - a rare form of Jade that bestows eternal life.<br /> <br /> The Jade Talisman Backgrounbd from the Kindred of the East book exactly this<br /> You own a talisman of true jade, jade attuned to the energies of Yin or Yang. Such a talisman can come in any shape or form \u2014 perhaps it\u2019s a figurine, a ring, a circlet, or even the guard of a sword. Talismans attuned to Yin tend to be fashioned from black or white jade, while talismans attuned to Yang are typically made of green, red, or blue jade. In any event, the talisman can store energies from either the Yin or Yang World (never both), then offer them to its wielder as the appropriate type of Chi. Whenever a jade talisman is an area where the Wall is weak (6 or lower), the player can choose to make a roll for the talisman, using a dice pool equal to the number of dots in this Background. The difficulty of this roll equals the Wall rating + 3. For each success, the talisman may absorb one Chi Point from the region\u2019s ambient Chi. Only one attempt to absorb Chi may be made per night, and a talisman may hold a maximum number of Chi Points equal to the dots in the Background (so, a three-dot jade talisman allows three dice to absorb Chi and may store a maximum of three Chi points at once). Subsequently, by touching the talisman and spending a turn in concentration, the vampire may utilize this Chi. Chi from a talisman may be used in normal fashion, to power Disciplines, heal wounds, etc. The vampire may still spend only as much Chi per turn as his Dharma rating allows. Talismans of true jade are exceedingly rare and greatly prized. You may have to defend your talisman against rapacious rivals. Occasionally, talismans are used as prizes in duels and gambling contests, but these contests must be of great consequence to merit such a stake.<br /> \u2022 One Chi Point<br /> \u2022\u2022 Two Chi Points<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022 Three Chi Points<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 Four Chi Points<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 Five Chi Points\u200b<br /> <br /> Land of Eight Million Dreams detailed the idea about Jade weapons; just as the Kithain are sensitive to Cold Iron, the shinma/hsien, the &quot;Little Gods&quot; can be sensitive to Jade. If the proper ritual awakens the chi of the jade it can cause aggravated wounds to the hsien.<br /> It should be stated that even ordinary mortals, even with no training, can attune jade through prayer and meditation. Jade is widely known to be of protection against spirits and the hisiem are still spirits even if they are trapped in human bodies.<br /> The Shih (the demon hunters of the Middle Kingdom) know this and often carry jade weapons.<br /> However, jade is also a power item for hsien - it can increase their magic, and many powerful treasures are made of Jade. Slightly paradoxical - but it works. Basically what it comes down to is - Jade has power. Anything with power is a two edged sword, this is why they have Jade Talisman: True Jade in Land of Eight Million Dreams<br /> Hengeyokai: Shapeshifters of the East mentions a few Jade Fetish or Talens but actually these are like other Werewolf things, somethat that is made out of Jade and atuned with a ritual or gift.<br /> <br /> In short, Jade is... well jade somebody properly atuned, you can buy it anywhere as a mineral, have an artisan to carve it and if you know the ritual, atune it<br /> The confusion\u200b comes from Wraith: The Oblivion:<br /> <b>White Jade</b> of Wraith is a &quot;Jade&quot; manufactured from the plasm of ghosts in the Yellow Springs. It is used to manufacture Artifacts in the same way that soulforging is used in Stygia. White Jade is made through a specific skill in the Way of the Artisan arcanos, and can only be created out of a wraith drained of all Willpower. Artifacts constructed from White Jade are less durable than artifacts constructed from <b>True Jade</b>, but it is far easier to acquire and therefore far cheaper.<br /> True Jade is is a more solid and durable form of Jade found in the Dark Kingdom of Jade, it is basically Relic Jade - a relic is a memory given shape in the Underworld that can survive for centuries. In this case some Jade. But compared to the white Jade made from plasm, even with the added relegious and cultural significance of Jade... it is rare. Very rare. Not to mention It is similar to Stygian Steel and causes aggravated damage...<br /> <br /> EDIT:<br /> Personally I tend to use a bit of a hosue rule to seperate the two kind of True Jade<br /> The Wraith True Jade is a relic of a Jade<br /> The &quot;mortal&quot; True Jade is a Jade that Jade spirit been &quot;awakened&quot; with a ritual","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","user":"Shadeprowler","date":". 08-23-2023, 08:45 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-23T05:39:45-07:00","id":1521250,"title":"","content":"One little add on, in the Dharma Book: Devil Tigers, there is a mention of the background &quot;Demon Jade Talisman&quot; which stores Demon Chi rather than yang or yin chi. The demon jade is translucent black in color. Though the source for the Demon Jade is the Yomi Hells themselves which last I checked is separate from the Wraith setting of Stygia.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Dat